title: Buchenwald, April 11, 1945
new-page: true

You awake, the sirens are wrong. For four years these sirens have meant Appel, get up, get out, stand in block, be counted. 

Not today. the sound comes from the direction of the gatehouse. Not the SS pattern, ragged, shouting, screaming. You lie o nyour plank, typhus hot. 

You do the math, as you have for the last two years. How many calories do I have, how many days can I stand, who in this block is dying?

The door of the block bangs open, a man you know, a Communist fellow, red triangle, transferred from Luckau opens out, shouting, German and Czech "The SS are gone, we have the tower, get up, get up!"

You cannot believe it. You have believed nothing for two years. But you get up, because getting up is what you do, and you walk out into the cold and the Appellplatz is full of men who are not standing in blocks. They stand in clumps, some fall onto the ground in tears, weeping.

Some are crying, others are holding rifles from the armoury, pinting them at the gatehouse. The vermin must be in there.

That man who entered your bloc, he has a list in his hands, a card index, distilled into columns. He is you. 

You watch as he takes to the bunker, the camp prison. The doors are open. inside the cells, are men, Kapos, informers, things like that. Men you know, men who beat you, who took bread, who wroet your name.

Well, now they are yours, these broken men are at the disposal of the Committee. You want to savage them, everyone does, some already have and no one cares, but there will be time later. You are thirty one, you weigh some forty five kilos, you have spent nine years in custody and now you are told to wriet the names of those who betrayed you.

It's hard. Your hand shakes, but you write, you write grudges, you write contempt, you write everyone. From a Kapo who stole my berad two years ago, to a man in Block 37 who was called out at night and came back with a sausage.

There's sixteen names when your hand gives out, you hand the paper back. "Good, thank you, Erdman," says he.

A night later, the Americans are coming up Ettersberg road. The engines are roaring, men are shouting in English and German. 

You walk outside, a red flag is hung from the gatehouse, beside it too is a white flag. 

You are standing, again, in the Appelplatz, where men you knew died standing, but no one stands in a block. 

You are standing next to the committee man, holding a pencil, with a list in your pocket of the men you have just denounced. The Americans are at the gate. The war is not over, but this war is. The war inside the wire.
